# ── Viewer (viewer-react) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Every name below is one the code actually reads — with one flagged exception,
# the SPA sign-in block near the end, which is read only by a module nothing
# calls. The previous block listed
# CESIUM_ION_TOKEN / GOOGLE_MAP_PLATFORM / MAPBOX_API_KEY / ESRI_* — the
# unprefixed names the removed vanilla viewer took through Vite `define`.
# Nothing has read those since that viewer was deleted, so following this file
# gave you a globe with no imagery, no buildings and no Street View.
#
# Vite only exposes variables prefixed VITE_, and inlines them at BUILD time,
# which also means every value here ends up **public** in the bundle. Put
# nothing in a VITE_ variable that you would not publish.
#
# All of them are optional: with none set, the globe falls back to Cesium's
# bundled offline Natural Earth II raster and the app still works.

# Imagery, terrain and 3D buildings.
VITE_CESIUM_ION_TOKEN=            # ion World Imagery/Terrain/OSM Buildings — ion.cesium.com
VITE_GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY=         # photorealistic 3D tiles + Street View stills
VITE_MAPBOX_TOKEN=                # sharper satellite/outdoors basemaps

# Open, keyless basemap overrides. Defaults are fine for development; set
# VITE_OSM_TILE_URL for any deployment, because the public OSM tile server's
# usage policy forbids production traffic.
VITE_EOX_TILE_URL=                # default https://tiles.maps.eox.at/wmts/1.0.0
VITE_EOX_S2_LAYER=                # default s2cloudless_3857 (2016, CC BY).
                                  # 2018+ layers are CC BY-NC-SA and cannot ship
                                  # in a commercial build — see basemaps.ts.
VITE_GIBS_LAYER=                  # default VIIRS_SNPP_CorrectedReflectance_TrueColor
VITE_GIBS_DATE=                   # YYYY-MM-DD; default is two UTC days back
VITE_OSM_TILE_URL=                # your own tile server

# Self-hosted terrain: a quantized-mesh tileset root with layer.json at it.
# See renderers/terrain.ts for what such a tileset must satisfy, including the
# EGM2008-to-ellipsoidal conversion that coastal pins depend on.
VITE_TERRAIN_URL=
VITE_TERRAIN_CREDIT=              # attribution your DEM requires

# Place search. Defaults to the public Photon instance; point at your own, or
# switch provider, for anything beyond development.
VITE_GEOCODER_URL=                # default https://photon.komoot.io/api
VITE_GEOCODER_PROVIDER=           # photon | nominatim | pelias (sniffed if unset)
VITE_GEOCODER_EMAIL=              # Nominatim only — its documented identification channel

# Companion Memory, as a build-time default. `?memory=`/`?token=` URL params
# override these per page load, so one built image can serve several servers.
# The token is inlined and public: use it only for single-tenant deployments.
VITE_MEMORY_URL=
VITE_MEMORY_TOKEN=

# Viewer SPA sign-in — NOT WIRED. The one exception to the promise at the top of
# this file, called out rather than quietly left in the list. `auth/portal.ts`
# reads these and implements the PKCE exchange correctly, but nothing calls it:
# there is no /auth/callback route and no sign-in control, and VITE_ATLAS_API has
# no reader anywhere in the viewer, so a completed login would not change what
# the app can fetch. Setting them today does nothing. The viewer authenticates by
# ambient CI-Server cookie, or by `?token=` / VITE_MEMORY_TOKEN above.
VITE_OIDC_ISSUER=                 # <portal-origin>/api/auth
VITE_OIDC_CLIENT_ID=companion-atlas
VITE_OIDC_REDIRECT_URI=           # e.g. http://localhost:4174/auth/callback
VITE_ATLAS_API=                   # e.g. http://localhost:8787/api

# Dev only: proxy Memory through the dev server to dodge CORS.
VITE_DEV_MEMORY_PROXY=

# ── Companion Atlas server (server/) ───────────────────────────────────────
# Where the published viewer and the API live (used to build share URLs).
ATLAS_VIEWER_BASE_URL=http://localhost:4174
ATLAS_API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8787/api

# Infrastructure (docker-compose provides these in-cluster).
DATABASE_URL=postgres://atlas:atlas@localhost:5432/atlas
REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379
S3_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:9000
S3_BUCKET=atlas

# ── Companion Intelligence suite connectors ────────────────────────────────
# CI-Server memory (GraphQL): seed stories from located memories + write stories
# back (createEvent). Origin or full endpoint — e.g. http://localhost:3030/graphql
# (direct dev) or https://ci-server.localhost/api/graphql (proxy). The Hub injects
# CI_MEMORY_URL + CI_MEMORY_TOKEN for a registered app.
CI_MEMORY_URL=
CI_MEMORY_TOKEN=
# OpenClaw / Hermes: suite agents that drive Atlas via MCP and receive results.
OPENCLAW_URL=
HERMES_URL=

# ── Companion OIDC (CI-Portal, auth for REST + MCP-over-HTTP) ──────────────
# Leave the issuer empty to disable auth locally (single dev subject).
# CI-Portal (better-auth) issuer is `<portal-origin>/api/auth`; JWKS derives as
# `<issuer>/jwks` (override with COMPANION_OIDC_JWKS_URI). `aud` = our client id.
# Public PKCE client (no secret). Local Portal dev origin: https://hub.ci.localhost
COMPANION_OIDC_ISSUER=
COMPANION_OIDC_JWKS_URI=
COMPANION_OIDC_AUDIENCE=companion-atlas
COMPANION_OIDC_CLIENT_ID=companion-atlas
ATLAS_DEV_SUBJECT=dev-operator
# The issuer-less bypass runs EVERY request as ATLAS_DEV_SUBJECT with `*` scope,
# so the server refuses to start that way unless the environment says it is not
# a deployment: NODE_ENV=development or test (the `dev` tasks set this), or this
# explicit opt-in. Set it to `1` only for staging/CI/demo boxes — never in
# production, where the fix is to configure COMPANION_OIDC_ISSUER instead.
ATLAS_ALLOW_DEV_AUTH=
